   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to opening of the polls",
    "basePrintNo": "S8152",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8152-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§8-102, 8-104, 8-202, 8-300, 8-302 & 8-312, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8152\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to opening of the polls\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend several sections within article 8 of the\nelection law regarding the opening of the polls and the duties of\nelection inspectors when utilizing ballot scanners.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 8-102 of the election law to\nprovide for the opening of the polling place. It includes a\nrequirement: that a guard rail be established with respect to the\nprivacy booths, ballot marking devices and ballot scanners, as well as\nall secure storage containers; that privacy booths and ballot scanners\ncontain conspicuous notices on how to mark a ballot; that all storage\ncontainers be unlocked to verify they are empty and that the container\nholding unused ballots be inspected to confirm it contains nothing\nelse; that the ballot scanners and ballot marking devices are\ninspected to assure they are in working order; that the privacy booths\nare properly placed to preserve the secrecy of the vote; that the keys\nthat are delivered match the machines in the polling place; that the\ncounters are set to zero when the ballot scanner is turned on; and\nthat the inspectors call the board of elections if the counters are\nnot set to zero.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends section 8-104 of the election law to\nprovide for posting instructions in the polling place on how to use a\nballot scanner and a ballot marking device and how to vote on an\nElection Day paper ballot. The section also mandates that the election\ninspectors be personally responsible for the secure ballot containers\nand that such containers not be opened except upon the instructions of\nthe board of elections.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends section 8-202 of the election law to\nrequire election inspectors to: attend the ballot scanners, ballot\nmarking devices, privacy booths and any secure storage containers;\nplace ballot scanner, ballot marking devices and privacy booths at\nleast 4 feet from the table used by inspectors in charge of the poll\nbooks; prohibit anyone from being closer than 3 feet from a ballot\nscanner, ballot marking device or privacy booth when in use by another\nperson; and monitor that the ballot scanners, ballot marking device\nand privacy booths are not tampered with or damaged.  Section 4 of the\nbill amends section 8-300 of the election law to provide that\nelections inspectors must ascertain that a voter is entitled to vote\nbefore they are permitted to use a ballot scanner, ballot marking\ndevice or privacy booth.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends section 8-300 of the election law to\nprovide that only 2 voters shall be allowed into the voting area at a\ntime for any privacy booth, ballot marking device or ballot Scanner\nafter ascertaining that the voters are entitled to vote; that the use\n\n\nof a privacy booth or ballot marking device shall be secret; and that\na voter may only use a privacy booth or a ballot scanner a reasonable\namount of time.\n\nSection 5 of the bill amends section 8-302 of the election law to\ncorrect a typographical error that refers to an emergency ballot in a\nprovision that addresses affidavit ballots.\n\nSection 6 of the bill amends section 8-312 of the election law to\nprovide how a voter marks a ballot and scans such ballot. It also\nprovides that upon the spoiling of a ballot, a voter may receive a new\nballot, up to a total of 3. It requires directions to the voter that\nhe or she should wait for the notice on the ballot scanner that the\nballot has been successfully scanned and, if such notice does not\nappear, that the voter shall seek the assistance of an election\ninspector. Finally, this section provides that no ballot without the\nofficial endorsement shall be deposited in a scanner or, if a scanner\nis unavailable, in a secure ballot container except for emergency\nballots as provided in section 7-120 of the election law.\n\nSection 7 of the bill contains the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe State of New York is scheduled to begin to use polling place\nballot scanners beginning with the Primary Election in September 2010\nin compliance with the federal Help America Vote Act and enabling\nstate legislation. This bill codifies the procedures for opening the\npolling place on Election Day and the duties of the election\ninspectors. The bill continues procedures that have been in place in\nNew York for many years regarding the opening of the polls and\nelection inspector duties except where the features of the new\nmachines warrant a change in language or substance.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
